From: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: reset the PCIe link on recovery power-up

Three ramoops-captured panics on a GL.iNet GL-BE9300 (IPQ5332 +
QCN9274) show that after an RDDM firmware crash any BAR access to the
endpoint can raise an Asynchronous SError on the SoC NoC - the
endpoint answers config space (so pci_device_is_present() passes) but
stalls memory space while it self-resets out of RDDM. The vendor
driver survives the same crash by performing a controlled link reset
(PERST toggle) and gating every register access on link-down flags;
mainline-style hosts just read ~0.

Do the equivalent with generic PCI core machinery: on every power-up
after the first, run pci_reset_bus() on the endpoint - a secondary
bus reset with config state saved and restored around it - so MHI
restart always starts from a freshly retrained link and a clean
device, never a half-dead one. First boot skips the reset (the device
just enumerated). If the reset fails, bail out cleanly and let the
group-recovery retry handle it instead of panicking the system.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.h
@@ -122,6 +122,7 @@ struct ath12k_pci {
 	unsigned long mhi_state;
 	enum mhi_callback mhi_pre_cb;
 	u32 register_window;
+	bool powered_up_once;
 
 	/* protects register_window above */
 	spinlock_t window_lock;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
@@ -1434,6 +1434,30 @@ int ath12k_pci_power_up(struct ath12k_ba
 	struct ath12k_pci *ab_pci = ath12k_pci_priv(ab);
 	int ret;
 
+	/* Recovery from an RDDM firmware crash: the endpoint self-resets
+	 * and stalls memory-space transactions while its link retrains.
+	 * On the IPQ5332 NoC a BAR access in that window raises a fatal
+	 * Asynchronous SError instead of returning ~0, so a radio crash
+	 * panics the whole system. Reset the link with config state
+	 * saved/restored (pci_reset_bus) before touching any register,
+	 * mirroring the vendor driver's PERST-based recovery.
+	 */
+	if (ab_pci->powered_up_once) {
+		ret = pci_reset_bus(ab_pci->pdev);
+		if (ret) {
+			ath12k_err(ab, "PCIe link reset failed: %d\n", ret);
+			return ret;
+		}
+
+		if (!pci_device_is_present(ab_pci->pdev)) {
+			ath12k_err(ab, "PCIe device absent after link reset\n");
+			return -ENODEV;
+		}
+
+		pci_set_master(ab_pci->pdev);
+	}
+	ab_pci->powered_up_once = true;
+
 	ab_pci->register_window = 0;
 	clear_bit(ATH12K_PCI_FLAG_INIT_DONE, &ab_pci->flags);
 	ath12k_pci_sw_reset(ab_pci->ab, true);
